Z||000000||HAGS||01-28-2001||04:46 PM||||Recharged the battery and tried to start again, it cranks but doesnt start...crank sounds the same as when it was working.  Some people say starter needs replacing, some people say it cant be the starter since it cranks...anyone know what else it could be?  The alternator is about 3 miles old...replaced the alternor, charged the battery and the car stalled on the way home, it wouldnt re-start, after about 12-15 tries the battery died.  I pushed it home since it was around the corner, charged the battery and tried again, still no start.||||reg||1
Z||000001||TheHans||01-28-2001||05:12 PM||||Try your fuel filter.<BR>Then your fuel pressure.<BR>Sounds like you have spark. Check anyway, There's a lot in the Archives. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||01-28-2001||05:49 PM||||You need fuel, air, and spark. If the engine's turning over and it sounds about right, pull a spark plug and ground it to the block t osee if you have spark.  Check your fuel pressure to make sure you're getting fuel.  Try fuel filter.  If you don't get spark, check the coil wires.  I've had my coil + wire ground out against the engine block and it just shuts the engine off.  Everything sounds fine, but it won't fire.  Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>When you choke a smurf, what color does it turn?||||reg||3
Z||000003||HAGS||01-28-2001||06:07 PM||||Will do, after the superbowl  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||01-28-2001||06:36 PM||||Anymore info on how all the problem started? May be a fuse link to the brain box.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DavidHenshaw||01-28-2001||07:22 PM||||When the starter turns the engine over but the engine won't start its almost always an ignition/spark problem. To be sure that it is in fact a spark problem ground one of the spark plug wires aginst the block (hold it about 1/8 inch away from the block) while someone turns the engine over. If there is no spark jumping across the gap you know its ingition.<P>An ignition problem is usually caused by a bad ignition module. The ignition module is found just under the distributor. Remove it and have it checked at an auto parts store. If the IM is ok I would then suspect the coil. Have it checked. If the coil is ok then its often a bad distributor cap rotor or the distributor cap itself or as it was in my car a bad distributor. Its unusual, but we recently had a member with a defective cam that wasn't causing the distributor to turn as it is supposed to do.<P>To eliminate the possibility of it being a fuel problem you can spray an aerosol can of "engine start" into the air intake as someone turns the engine over. If the spray causes the engine to run momentairly then you know you have a fuel problem. My money is on ignition problem however.<P>Dave <P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||HAGS||01-28-2001||08:30 PM||||Well after I got the battery from the shop the first time it started up fine and I drove it about a mile, stopped and went to go look at my schedule to see when I worked...then drove another mile, got some food, and then started home, stalled at a light on the third mile....would crank but not start...both stops before I stopped my engine and restarted fine.  Spark plugs are new and lasted me the 100 mile trip up to Austin...im guessing I screwed something up when I put the alternator in, 02 sensor needs replacing, no codes show up as im trying to start it.  I cant remember much of what happend at the light because it was green and I was being rushed by the people behind but I think I got the feeling it ran out of gas, I had died on 1/4 tank before so I got the gas can out of the back and spilled it all over the car since the top came off but managed to get about half a gallon in there to no avail. <BR>I was gonna mention that my lifters are knocking but didnt think that would cause it, but then you said some guy had a problem with his cam being what was causing it not to start.  When the knocking started so did smoke from the rocker arm cover...checked the oil and it showed a quart low I believe, added some knock stop stuff to it and it stopped the smoking but not the knocking.  <BR>I think thats everything, can you describe how to test the spark plugs a little more, as I picture it now I take them out one at a time and hold them to the engine and crank the car?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||01-28-2001||09:06 PM||||Just pull 1 spark plug and ground it to the block, have some one crank the car and see if you see spark jumping the gap. If not, you have ignition problems. If yes, you have fuel problems. <P>Also, I would reccomend you dont hold the spark plug itself! Could be a little painful. Although, we do have a teacher at school who checks his timing like that on a very old Ford Ranger, bet he wouldnt do that on a 40K+ Volt ingition system like mine.<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 01-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||HAGS||01-28-2001||10:27 PM||||Got no spark, what I did was pull the plug out and reconnect the wire to it, held the big part of it to the rocker arm cover and got no spark, tried it in different spots on the engine to no avail.....would a disconnected alternator cause this? As I was waiting for my brother to come start it I noticed that the wires to the alternator were missing.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Indiana_resto_guy||01-28-2001||10:49 PM||||I would have to say to run those wires down first and reconnect them. (OK, so that's a no brainer-sorry) Then be sure that the big red wire still has +12 volts to it. Does your battery light/alternator gauge read correctly?<BR>Try to start . . . no start? Read on.<BR>Next, check all your fuse links and make sure they are good. If you have a Haynes manual, look in the back of the book at the wireing diagrams under "power distribution". This will tell you what circuts operate which section of the electrical system. Concentrate on the things that involve the engine power. This should get you on the right track. Also, change out your inginition module as it may have gotten fried by a power surge if the wires off the alternator were grounded. Hopefully there is no damage to the computer.||||reg||1
Z||000010||triker||01-28-2001||11:04 PM||||Get your coil tested.  Even if you're getting spark from a plug wire to the block, it may not be enough to fire under compression.  The spark should jump at least 1/2 inch and a good coil will make it jump 3/4 of an inch.  I had the same prob awhile back and a mechanic friend of mine tested my coil and it was only putting out about half of what it should.  I would never have thought it was the prob since I was 'getting spark'.||||reg||1
Z||000011||lou_dias||01-29-2001||07:36 AM||||Saturday night my car just died on the highway.  My first hint was the rpms went dead but I know my engine was still turning because I was in gear.  Got to the break down lane and try starting it up again.  Cranked fine, just didn't start.  Got towed home.  After the tow truck dropped my car off, I cranked it again and it ran for 10 seconds.  All the belts were fine so I figured it was the infamous ignition control module.  Bought one at 8:30 pm at a local Autozone.  At 11am the next morning, I replaceD the ICM and it started right away and I drove 70 miles to my friend's house for a Superbowl party.<BR>It cost me $31.99 + tax and came with an 83 year/999 month warranty.  I noticed that the heat transfer gel on my old ICM wasn't spread out across the entire surface of the heat transfer plate.  When you replace the ICM make sure you or you mechanic spread that white gel all over the metal surface.  This is the same stuff that you put on a CPU when you add a heatsink and fan to it.  It transfers heat from the ICM to the distributor to keep the ICM cool and prevent heat damage which is why it failed in the first place.  This was stated in the instructions that came with the ICM but obviously the last guy who put in my ICM didn't pay attention to detail.||||reg||1
Z||000012||HAGS||01-30-2001||10:05 AM||||My car started!!!  Everyone thinks im crazy but all it took was me sitting on the trunk for 10 minutes just talking to her...I told her I was trying to fix her but she would have to co-operate or I'd have to part her out, I went to see if I could hear the fuel pump running when I turn the key on and figured what the hell, I'll try to crank again and it started!  So after 3 days of "what the hells" one worked!  I hope I dont regret turning her off to come tell you guys but there is one problem, I cant find my alternator wires! They arent attached and I cant see them anywhere!||||reg||1
